/-!
# The equality criterion for the relative group of a full functor

Let `T : C ⥤ D` be an additive functor between preadditive categories, `C` having binary
biproducts, and let `K₀(T)` be its relative Grothendieck group (`TauCeti.RelativeK0`), generated by
the triples `(P, α, Q)` with `α : T P ≅ T Q`. Adding an identity triple `(S, id, S)` does not change
the class of a triple, so stably isomorphic triples have the same class. When `T` is **full** the
converse holds: two triples `t` and `t'` have the same class in `K₀(T)` exactly when

```text
t ⊞ (S, id, S) ≅ t' ⊞ (S', id, S')
```

for some objects `S` and `S'` of `C`, chosen independently (`TauCeti.RelativeK0.of_eq_of_iff`).
In particular `[t] = 0` iff `t ⊞ (S, id, S) ≅ (S', id, S')` for some `S` and `S'`
(`TauCeti.RelativeK0.of_eq_zero_iff`). The two stabilizers cannot be taken equal: for the identity
functor of finite-dimensional vector spaces the triples `(0, id, 0)` and `(k, id, k)` both have
class zero, but adding the same `S` to both leaves dimensions that differ by one.

Fullness cannot be dropped. Base change from finitely generated free `ℤ`-modules to `ℚ`-modules
is not full, and the triple `(ℤ², diag(2, 1/2), ℤ²)` has class `(ℤ, 2, ℤ) + (ℤ, 1/2, ℤ) = 0`. It is
not stably isomorphic to an identity triple, since `diag(2, 1/2) ⊕ 1` would then be the image of an
integral matrix. Base change of finitely generated projective modules along a surjective ring
homomorphism, such as `R → R/I`, is full (`RingHom.full_finiteProjectiveModulesExtendScalars`), so
the criterion applies to the relative group `K₀(R, I)` of a ring and a two-sided ideal.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.RelativeK0.tripleBiprodTransIso`: for a full additive `T`, the isomorphism
  `(P, α, Q) ⊞ (Q, β, S) ≅ (P, β ∘ α, S) ⊞ (Q, id, Q)` behind the composition relation.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.RelativeK0.of_eq_of_iff`: for a full additive `T`, two triples have the same class iff
  they are stably isomorphic.
* `TauCeti.RelativeK0.of_eq_zero_iff`: for a full additive `T`, a triple has class zero iff it is
  stably isomorphic to an identity triple.

## Implementation notes

The stable isomorphism classes of triples form an abelian group under direct sum, and the
composition relation `(P, β ∘ α, S) = (P, α, Q) + (Q, β, S)` holds there; the universal property of
`K₀(T)` then gives a homomorphism to this group sending `[t]` to the stable class of `t`. Fullness
enters through the composition relation: `(P, α, Q) ⊞ (Q, β, S)` and `(P, β ∘ α, S) ⊞ (Q, id, Q)`
are isomorphic through the identity of `P ⊞ Q` and an isomorphism `Q ⊞ S ≅ S ⊞ Q` of `C` whose
image under `T` is the diagonal sum of `β` and `β⁻¹`. By fullness `β = T b` and `β⁻¹ = T c`, and
Whitehead's isomorphism `CategoryTheory.Biprod.whiteheadIso b c` has this image although `b` and
`c` need not be inverse to each other.

## References

* C. A. Weibel, *The K-book: An Introduction to Algebraic K-Theory*, Chapter II, Definition 2.10
  and Exercise 2.17 (the relative group of a cofinal functor), and Chapter III, Lemma 1.3.3
  (Whitehead's lemma).
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

